  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 237 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: First forecast challenge is areal coverage/duration of \nfog after midnight tonight. Sfc analysis shows 1022mb high pres over \nthe thumb of eastern MI, as a sfc cold frnt exits eastern New \nEngland. GOES-19 water vapor imagery shows large axis of dry air \naloft moving acrs the Great Lakes into the NE CONUS this aftn, which \nshould help quickly erode cloud cover by this evening, as sfc \nheating/instability is decreasing. Sounding data indicates modest \ncaa and dry air advection continues thru 04z, before northwest flow \nweakens and shallow inversion strengthens. This sharpening inversion \nwith clearing skies and light winds should allow for development of \npatchy dense fog with highest probability (80-90%) in favored mtn \nvalleys. Temps wl range from the upper 30s SLK to lower/mid 50s near \nLake Champlain.\n\nOn Saturday 1024mb high pres will build toward KBGM with deep dry \nlayer shifting toward central New England by 18z, as a pocket of \nslightly better moisture in the 1000-500mb layer moves overhead. \nThis combined with some weak instability wl help produce some \nshallow fair wx cumulus clouds, especially acrs the mtns. Progged \n925mb temps btwn 14-16C support highs mainly in the 70s with \ncomfortable humidity values. Saturday night is quiet with some \npatchy fog, but less than Friday night, as 925mb to 850mb southwest \nflow develops with some increasing moisture advection from west to \neast. Lows wl be warmer with values in the upper 40s to near 60F.\n\nSunday/Sunday night the forecast challenge wl be potential for \nshowers and a few embedded thunderstorms as weak front approaches, \nalong with s/w energy. Synoptic scale setup shows fast confluent 700-\n500mb flow btwn closed cyclonic circulation over eastern Canada and \nBermuda Ridge over the western Atlantic as weak channeled vortices \nmoves acrs our cwa. Pw values range btwn 1.25 and 1.4\", which is \naround 1 STD above normal ahead of approaching cold front near the \nInternational Border, while best instability is south of our cwa. \nLatest trends have shown less areal coverage of precip and mostly \nconfined to the higher trrn on Sunday aftn, especially with 925 to \n700mb southwest downslope flow here in the CPV. As winds shift to a \nfavorable north/northwest upslope flow behind boundary a period of \nlight upslope rain showers wl be possible on Sunday night, \nespecially northern VT mtns into the eastern CPV. Highs Sunday will \nbe in the 70s with slightly higher humidity with lows in the 50s to \nnear 60F again on Sunday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The latest suite of global guidance is bringing high \npressure across the Canadian Maritimes further south which is now \nexpected to suppress much of the rainfall activity on Tuesday to our \nsouth. There is still the potential we could see models shift north \nslightly over the next few days as upper air analysis begins to \nsample several low amplitude features in the longwave trough. \nHowever, the idea that widespread rainfall across the region on \nTuesday looks unlikely and the latest forecast has shows decreased \nrain chances as a result. The ECMWF and GFS both remain in good \nagreement with a series of frontal boundaries moving across the \nregion Wednesday into Thursday with widespread rainfall likely. Deep \nlayer ridging then looks poised to develop across the region with \ndrier conditions expected Friday into next weekend.",
